<header><p class="st-kicker">crusader state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Sidon</h1><p class="st-meta">1110 CE – 1260 CE · capital Sidon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the four great baronies of the Kingdom of Jerusalem, whose lords built Sidon's Sea Castle and the fortress of Beaufort.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sidon">Sidon</a> surrendered in December 1110 to Baldwin I, helped by the Norwegian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigurd-the-crusader">Sigurd the Crusader</a> and his fleet. The city was granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eustace-grenier">Eustace Grenier</a>, and his descendants held it as one of the kingdom's chief lordships.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lordship included the coast between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/beirut">Beirut</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tyre">Tyre</a> and inland castles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaufort-castle-lebanon">Beaufort</a> above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/litani-river">Litani</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reynald-of-sidon">Reynald of Sidon</a> spoke Arabic and was respected by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, with whom he negotiated while holding Beaufort in 1189-1190. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon-sea-castle">Sea Castle</a> on an island in the harbour was built in 1228. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> and trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> supported the economy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Lost in 1187 and recovered in the 13th century, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sidon">Sidon</a> was sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1260, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-grenier">Julian Grenier</a> sold it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a>, who held it until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> took it in 1291.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="crusader state, 1110 CE – 1260 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">crusader state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.79%;width:2.99%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1110 CE – 1260 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>crusader state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sidon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Eustace Grenier, Reynald of Sidon, Julian Grenier</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Latin Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Seigneurie of Sidon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1185&amp;focus=lordship-of-sidon" aria-label="Lordship of Sidon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5583 960 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Sidon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5903 1216L5908 1277"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5908 1277h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5903 1216h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5903 1216h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sidon. <a href="/atlas?year=1185&amp;focus=lordship-of-sidon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

